WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.041
A fraud rule allows five attempts per card per minute.

2
00:00:06.041 --> 00:00:10.991
Every pod adds one to the same Redis key, and the count starts over each minute.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.590
That's a fixed window.

4
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.760
Five attempts land in the last second of minute zero, and fill it.

5
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:21.605
At the boundary the count resets, and minute one lets five more attempts in.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.617
The rule said five a minute.

7
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:27.036
The card got ten in 1.4 seconds.

8
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.180
Some clients burst on purpose.

9
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.397
A merchant's refund job sends forty requests at once, every hour.

10
00:00:33.397 --> 00:00:36.576
You want a burst of up to ten, then two a second.

11
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.537
A token bucket does that.

12
00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:43.777
The bucket holds at most ten tokens, and gains one every 500 milliseconds.

13
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:45.738
Each request takes a token.

14
00:00:45.738 --> 00:00:47.543
A burst of fifteen arrives.

15
00:00:47.543 --> 00:00:53.425
The first ten take every token, and the last five find the bucket empty and are refused.

16
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:57.299
Now one request comes every 250 milliseconds.

17
00:00:57.299 --> 00:01:02.244
A token arrives every 500, so every other request gets through.

18
00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:03.813
That's two a second.

19
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:05.906
Nothing drips tokens in.

20
00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:14.337
Redis keeps two numbers per merchant, in one hash: the tokens left after the last request, and ts, when it last refilled.

21
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:24.299
On each request, a script counts the whole 500 millisecond steps since ts, adds that many tokens, never more than ten, and takes one.

22
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:27.317
Two pods ask at once, with one token left.

23
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:32.203
If each one reads and then writes, both see one token, and both take it.

24
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.468
Redis runs the whole script as one command.

25
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:38.251
Pod A's runs first and takes the token.

26
00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:40.463
Pod B's sees zero, and refuses.

27
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:45.132
The key's time to live is five seconds, the time to refill from empty.

28
00:01:45.132 --> 00:01:49.405
A bucket quiet that long is full anyway, so Redis can drop the key.

29
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:55.039
A token bucket lets a quiet client spend a burst, then holds it to the refill rate.

30
00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:58.732
One script on one key keeps every pod on the same count.
